  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1207 AM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper-level trough will make its way into Montana today with\nthe accompanying surface cold front expected to bring gusty winds\nto the region along with a chance for afternoon showers and\nthunderstorms. These thunderstorms will be drier in nature which\nwill also increase fire weather concerns, particularly across\nSouthwest Montana, through the evening.\n\nBehind the trough, more zonal flow will set up allowing for\ntemperatures to remain relatively cooler at least over the next\ncouple days before upper-level ridging starts to build by the\nmiddle of the week. Lingering monsoonal moisture in the region \nalong with the chance for some additional moisture towards the end\nof the week may provide daily chances for afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms, especially for Southwest Montana.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms Sunday:\nWith ample CAPE and shear in place and the cold front moving \nthrough, there is decent support for thunderstorm develop across \nthe region this afternoon and evening. The remaining question is \nhow this event will play out. The 00Z suite of hi-res models, \nparticularly the HRRR, came in with much less coverage compared to\nprevious runs and between the remaining hi-res models there is \nstill some disagreement about whether there will be higher \ncoverage in Southwest Montana or Central Montana. Some models like\nthe NAM have pointed towards a threat of thunderstorms forming \noff the Little Belts and quickly becoming strong as they move into\nJudith Basin and Fergus county.\n\nWhile uncertainty remains in who will get what, there is\nconfidence that the majority of thunderstorm activity will move\noff into eastern Montana by 7 PM. Additionally the main hazards\nwill be frequent lightning and strong, gusty winds with hail being\na secondary hazard primarily with the initial storms that develop.\n-thor",
